# tests/qemu-boot.reboot.forbidden -- substrings that must NOT appear in the
# REBOOT's log: the second boot of the disk image a disk boot left behind
# (MINIXRS_VBLK_IMAGE=target/disk.img). Checked with
# tests/qemu-boot.reboot.expected by tools/check-boot-log.sh reboot <log>, and
# with nothing else -- the common forbidden set is NOT applied, for the reason
# that file's header gives. So a kernel panic in a reboot is not caught by this
# pair as such; it is caught by `persist gen=1` never being printed.

# The counter did not move: the image was booted, written, booted again, and
# still holds the `0` it shipped with. Either the first boot's rewrite never
# reached the device (spec mutation 9 skips it in init; a driver that
# acknowledged writes without issuing them looks the same), or this is not the
# image the first boot wrote -- the runner rebuilt it, or attached a copy.
# This line is REQUIRED of every full boot, which is the point: the reboot's
# set cannot be satisfied by a fresh boot, nor a fresh boot's by a reboot.
minix.rs init: persist gen=0
# The probe could not read the counter or could not store the next one; the
# common forbidden file names the steps. On a reboot `FAIL value` is the
# interesting one: the previous boot's truncate reached the device and its
# write did not (an empty file), or the file holds something that is not one
# decimal digit.
minix.rs init: persist FAIL
# The root did not come from the disk. `bdev.root vblk ... fallback=memory`:
# MFS found `vblk` and the driver served no root, so the counter above was read
# from the ramdisk (and would have been 0). `root FAIL <why>`: the driver
# declined the used image -- on a reboot the spellings that matter are `label`
# and `tail label`, a first boot that wrote outside the filesystem, and
# `gpt ...`, one that wrote outside the partition.
[diag mfs] bdev.root vblk
[diag vblk] root FAIL
